<!--
    GH-782 (kuna): a C declaration could not name a record before its body.
    `struct Node { struct Node *next; };` failed at the inner `struct Node`
    ("Identifier does not represent a struct as required"), as did a callback
    member taking the enclosing record, two records pointing at each other, and
    the forward declaration `typedef struct Node Node;`.  And a pointer built to
    a record before the record was completed (every self pointer, including a
    DWARF one) named the member-less stub, so a walk down a list printed
    `((int4 *)n->next)[2]` rather than `n->next->val`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P0 knowledge (the C-declaration grammar declares an
    unknown tag incomplete inside a member list or as a bare forward declaration)
    and P5 type propagation (a LOAD/STORE value typed as a pointer to a record's
    stub takes the pointer to the completed record).  No option: a strict fix.

    The fixture (SysV x86-64, clang -O2):

        second_val: mov rax,[rdi]; mov eax,[rax+8]          n->next->val
        third_val:  mov rax,[rdi]; mov rax,[rax];
                    mov eax,[rax+8]                         n->next->next->val
        a_b_a_x:    mov rax,[rdi]; mov rax,[rax];
                    mov rax,[rax+8]                         p->b->a->x
        a_b_y:      mov rax,[rdi]; mov eax,[rax+8]          p->b->y

    `Node` is declared ahead of its body, `Link` names itself inside its own
    body, and `A`/`B` point at each other with `B` first named inside `A`.
-->
